// IRIW in the release-acquire fragment.
//
// rmm-zoo property: mca (Atomicity); also the SC-vs-SRA ordering witness.
// Source: Lahav, Giannarakis & Vafeiadis, "Taming Release-Acquire Consistency"
//         (POPL 2016); Lahav & Boker, "What's Decidable about Causally
//         Consistent Shared Memory?" (TOPLAS 2022), Ex. 3.5.
//         Vendored shape: rmm-zoo litmus/strictly-weaker/SC-vs-SRA/IRIW.
//
// Release stores and acquire loads do not make writes multicopy atomic: the
// execution closes no (hb u mo) cycle, so even SRA -- the strongest model of the
// release-acquire family -- allows the two readers to disagree about the order
// of the two writes. Only a genuinely multicopy-atomic model (SC, TSO, ARMv8)
// forbids it.
//
// Reference verdicts: WRA, RA, SRA, C11, RC11 ALLOW. SC FORBIDS.
x := 0;
y := 0;
{
  x.store(1, rel)
} ||| {
  y.store(1, rel)
} ||| {
  r1 := x.load(acq);
  r2 := y.load(acq)
} ||| {
  r3 := y.load(acq);
  r4 := x.load(acq)
}
%%
allow (r1 = 1 && r2 = 0 && r3 = 1 && r4 = 0) [SRA]
